The problem is that Cypress has no idea you are making an assertion about an element - because you're not. You are making an assertion about a `String` and a `Number`.

That's why you cannot tell which element is failing because you're simply comparing strings and numbers.

If you were to rewrite your assertion to actually be about the element you would already have the tools in Cypress to see which one was failing.

```javascript
it('it should contain images in HTTPS with alt attribute', function () {
  cy
    .get('img')
    .each(function ($el) {
      // assertion which starts on the $el
      // and then changes to the return value of attr("src")
      expect($el).to.have.attr("src").and.match(/^https/)

      // assertion which starts on the $el 
      expect($el).to.have.attr("alt")
    });
});
```

Now in Cypress when you hover over the assertions about actual element you will see Cypress not only highlight the element on the page, but also when you click on the assertion you will see `console.log` information about the element in question.

